If you're learning Machine Learning from a software engineer's perspective, these are the 5 GitHub repositories I'd prioritize:

1. Scikit-Learn Examples
https://github.com/scikit-learn/scikit-learn?utm_source=chatgpt.com

Why: The industry-standard ML library for beginners.

Learn:

Linear Regression
Logistic Regression
Decision Trees
Random Forest
Feature Engineering
Model Evaluation

Start with:

examples/
2. Hands-On Machine Learning Notebooks (Aurelien Geron)
https://github.com/ageron/handson-ml3?utm_source=chatgpt.com

Why: Probably the best practical ML repository available.

Learn:

End-to-end ML projects
Scikit-Learn
TensorFlow
Feature Engineering
Model Deployment

For someone with your background, this is the best starting point.

3. Machine Learning From Scratch
https://github.com/eriklindernoren/ML-From-Scratch?utm_source=chatgpt.com

Why: Understand how algorithms work internally.

Build:

Linear Regression
Logistic Regression
KNN
Decision Trees
Random Forest
PCA

No heavy libraries hiding the math.

4. Microsoft ML for Beginners
https://github.com/microsoft/ML-For-Beginners?utm_source=chatgpt.com

Why: Structured course with notebooks and exercises.

Learn:

Supervised Learning
Unsupervised Learning
Classification
Regression
Clustering

Very beginner-friendly.

5. Awesome Machine Learning
https://github.com/josephmisiti/awesome-machine-learning?utm_source=chatgpt.com

Why: A curated list of the entire ML ecosystem.

Use it to discover:

Libraries
Courses
Books
Datasets
Tools

Think of it as the "directory" of ML.

Recommended Learning Order
Microsoft ML for Beginners
Hands-On Machine Learning
Scikit-Learn Examples
ML From Scratch
Awesome Machine Learning
For Your AI Engineer Goal

Given your React + Node + Python + FastAPI background, I'd follow:

Week 1-2:
Microsoft ML for Beginners

Week 3-4:
Hands-On Machine Learning

Week 5:
ML From Scratch

Week 6:
Build a project:
Study Hours -> Score Prediction
Customer Churn Prediction
House Price Prediction

Week 7:
Move to LLMs, RAG, LangChain, LangGraph

This path gets you to practical ML quickly without spending months on theory.